export const S2_HTML = "<p class=\"post-lede\">Everyone quotes the 2017 transformer paper as the spark of the modern boom. Read it against the fossil record and it looks more like a boundary — the Ediacaran–Cambrian line, where an entire way of doing AI quietly lost the ground it stood on.</p>\n\n<p class=\"drop-cap\">When I was small, I assumed every extinction needed a villain. The dinosaurs had their asteroid, and I took it for granted that the rest of the fossil record ran on the same logic: a catastrophe arrives from outside, a world ends. The museum's Ediacaran cases never held me as a kid — soft, quilted, faintly plant-like things, none of them chasing anything, none of them being chased. It took me years to understand that those quiet fronds were casualties of the single most consequential turnover in the history of animals, and that what killed them was, of all things, other animals learning a new way to live.</p>\n\n<p>Biologists still argue about how the Ediacaran ended. The candidates are a catastrophe, a biotic replacement, or a <em>taphonomic</em> disappearance — the slow business of bodies passing out of the biosphere and into the rock, a question of how death gets recorded rather than what caused it. The most careful recent work — Darroch et al. (2015) in <em>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</em>, and Mussini &amp; Dunn (2024) in <em>Biological Reviews</em> — comes down on biotic replacement. The Ediacarans were out-competed. The first burrowing animals churned up the microbial mats that their whole way of feeding depended on, and Darroch's name for those animals has stuck with me: <em>ecosystem engineers</em>. Organisms that rebuild the world so completely that the previous tenants can no longer live in it.</p>\n\n<p>The AI version of that story is clean enough that it took me a while to trust it. For roughly two decades before 2017, teaching a neural network to handle language meant teaching it to read in single file. Recurrent architectures processed a sequence one step at a time, carrying a hidden state forward from token to token and straining to hold onto anything that had happened more than a few words back. The lineage is easy to name: Elman's simple RNN (recurrent neural network) in 1990, then the LSTM — Long Short-Term Memory — from Hochreiter &amp; Schmidhuber in 1997, which tamed the vanishing-gradient problem well enough to become the field's workhorse for the better part of twenty years. It ran Google's production translation system. It had serious defenders. And it carried one constraint down its entire length: step <em>t</em> could not begin until step <em>t</em>&minus;1 had finished, so no part of the computation could be spread out across time.</p>\n\n<p>Beneath the recurrent models ran an older stratum of natural-language processing (NLP) that had been in production far longer — expert systems like MYCIN, Cyc, and DENDRAL; hand-built pipelines for tagging parts of speech and parsing dependencies; the statistical word-vector methods, TF-IDF and word2vec and GloVe, that turned language into geometry. Adolf Seilacher had a word for the Ediacaran seafloor these remind me of: <em>matground</em>. Flat, layered, quietly productive, and completely dependent on a world that held still.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">Two Substrates — The Ediacarans of NLP</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">The soft-bodied biota of the pre-transformer era, and the seafloor they map onto</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"comp\">\n    <div class=\"comp-bio\">\n      <div class=\"comp-side-lbl\">Ediacaran Biota (~571&ndash;539 Ma)</div>\n      <p><strong>Soft-bodied, matground-reliant.</strong> <em>Dickinsonia</em> (up to 1.4 m of quilted pancake), <em>Charnia</em> (fern-frond), <em>Kimberella</em> (a possible early mollusk) — grazing microbial films, with no mouths, no guts, and nothing to prey on them. Seilacher filed the whole clade under \"Vendobionta,\" a failed experiment in body-planning.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"comp-ai\">\n      <div class=\"comp-side-lbl\">Pre-Transformer NLP (1990&ndash;2016)</div>\n      <p><strong>Recurrent lineage:</strong> RNN (Elman, 1990) &middot; LSTM (Hochreiter &amp; Schmidhuber, 1997) &middot; GRU, Gated Recurrent Unit (Cho et al., 2014) &middot; seq2seq (Sutskever et al., 2014) &middot; soft attention (Bahdanau et al., 2015).</p>\n      <p><strong>Matground systems:</strong> expert systems (MYCIN, Cyc, DENDRAL) &middot; part-of-speech tagging and dependency parsing &middot; TF-IDF, word2vec, GloVe (Global Vectors for Word Representation).</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"comp-note\">The shared failure mode: a matground grazer was organized around a seafloor that never moved, and a recurrent model was organized around step <em>t</em> waiting on step <em>t</em>&minus;1. Remove the still floor, or the sequential dependency, and neither lineage has a fallback — the thing you took away was load-bearing.</p>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Pre-transformer language models, lined up against the Ediacaran biota: real, defended, production-grade, and built around a dependency that could not survive being removed. Sources: Elman 1990; Hochreiter &amp; Schmidhuber 1997; Cho et al. 2014; Sutskever et al. 2014; Bahdanau et al. 2015; Seilacher's Vendobionta hypothesis; Darroch et al. 2015.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Attention, and What It Actually Changed</h3>\n\n<p>Then, in 2017, eight authors at Google — Vaswani, Shazeer, Parmar, Uszkoreit, Jones, Gomez, Kaiser, and Polosukhin — published \"Attention Is All You Need\" (NeurIPS 2017; arXiv:1706.03762). Fewer than twelve pages, ostensibly a paper about machine translation; it beat the prior state of the art on the WMT 2014 English&ndash;German benchmark, scored in BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy). What it actually did was throw out recurrence and convolution together. Self-attention — softmax(QK<sup>T</sup>/&radic;d<sub>k</sub>)V — lets every token in a sequence attend to every other token at once. The cost is O(n&sup2;) in the sequence length; the payoff is that the whole operation collapses into matrix multiplies a GPU can run in parallel across the entire sequence.</p>\n\n<p>Here is the change in plain terms. Before \"Attention Is All You Need,\" a model read a sentence the way you read a scroll you can only unroll from one end — one word at a time, left to right, each word waiting on the one before it. The transformer let the model take in the whole scroll at once and decide, position by position, which other positions mattered. Three shifts made that work: reading context in parallel instead of in series, a self-attention mechanism for weighting which context is relevant, and a tokenizer that gave the model a cleaner unit to hold in memory. The move from a recurrent architecture to a feedforward one is the heart of it — and it is worth being exact about what went parallel, because the loose version of this claim is simply wrong.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <div class=\"callout-tag\">A Precise Correction — From Recurrent to Feedforward</div>\n  <p>Be careful about which half went parallel. Generation is still serial: a language model decodes autoregressively, one token at a time, each new token conditioned on everything already written. The parallelism lives in <strong>context and training</strong>. During pretraining, and while a model ingests a prompt, every position in the sequence can be handled simultaneously, because step <em>t</em> no longer waits on step <em>t</em>&minus;1. Parallel context, serial generation. That gap is where the scaling comes from.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">Recurrent vs. Self-Attention</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">Why removing the step-by-step dependency was the unlock</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\">\n    <svg viewBox=\"0 0 960 300\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;max-width:100%;\" font-family=\"DM Sans, system-ui, sans-serif\">\n      <rect width=\"960\" height=\"300\" fill=\"white\"/>\n      <defs>\n        <marker id=\"ah\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\">\n          <path d=\"M0 0 L6 3 L0 6 Z\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n        </marker>\n      </defs>\n      <line x1=\"480\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"480\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#e5e0d8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <text x=\"40\" y=\"42\" fill=\"#6b6b6b\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.5\">RECURRENT — SERIAL</text>\n      <g>\n        <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n        <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n        <line x1=\"310\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"120\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#89a7cc\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"120\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#89a7cc\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"120\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#89a7cc\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"400\" cy=\"120\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#89a7cc\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n        <text x=\"70\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\">h&#8321;</text>\n        <text x=\"180\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\">h&#8322;</text>\n        <text x=\"290\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\">h&#8323;</text>\n        <text x=\"400\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\">h&#8324;</text>\n      </g>\n      <g stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\">\n        <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"145\"/>\n        <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"145\"/>\n        <line x1=\"290\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"145\"/>\n        <line x1=\"400\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"145\"/>\n      </g>\n      <g font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2c2c2c\">\n        <rect x=\"46\" y=\"178\" width=\"48\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f0ebe2\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The</text>\n        <rect x=\"156\" y=\"178\" width=\"48\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f0ebe2\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\">cat</text>\n        <rect x=\"266\" y=\"178\" width=\"48\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f0ebe2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\">sat</text>\n        <rect x=\"376\" y=\"178\" width=\"48\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f0ebe2\"/><text x=\"400\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\">down</text>\n      </g>\n      <text x=\"40\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#6b6b6b\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-style=\"italic\">Step t waits on step t&minus;1. Computation cannot be parallelized across time.</text>\n      <text x=\"540\" y=\"42\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.5\">SELF-ATTENTION — PARALLEL CONTEXT</text>\n      <g stroke=\"#6286bf\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.55\">\n        <path d=\"M600 118 Q650 70 700 118\"/>\n        <path d=\"M700 118 Q750 70 800 118\"/>\n        <path d=\"M800 118 Q850 70 900 118\"/>\n        <path d=\"M600 118 Q700 48 800 118\"/>\n        <path d=\"M700 118 Q800 48 900 118\"/>\n        <path d=\"M600 118 Q750 28 900 118\"/>\n      </g>\n      <g font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2c2c2c\">\n        <rect x=\"576\" y=\"118\" width=\"48\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/><text x=\"600\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2c2c2c\">The</text>\n        <rect x=\"676\" y=\"118\" width=\"48\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/><text x=\"700\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2c2c2c\">cat</text>\n        <rect x=\"776\" y=\"118\" width=\"48\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2c2c2c\">sat</text>\n        <rect x=\"876\" y=\"118\" width=\"48\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/><text x=\"900\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2c2c2c\">down</text>\n      </g>\n      <text x=\"540\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-style=\"italic\">Every token attends to every other, at once — one matrix multiply, fully GPU-parallel.</text>\n    </svg>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">The recurrent chain (left) forces a sequential dependency; self-attention (right) replaces it with an all-to-all operation computed in parallel across the sequence. Generation stays autoregressive — it is the <em>context</em> pass that becomes parallel. Source: Vaswani et al., \"Attention Is All You Need,\" 2017.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>Reading it now, the striking thing is how modestly it was framed. This was a translation model, presented as a translation model. The translation record turned out to be almost incidental to why the paper mattered.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"pull\">\n  Self-attention made scaling along the compute axis parallel — and parallel is the only kind of scaling a chip, a cluster, or a capital market knows how to reward.\n</blockquote>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">The Long Fuse</h3>\n\n<p>What came next was Erwin's \"macroevolutionary lag\" running backwards. In the Cambrian, the developmental toolkit — the Hox clusters, the regulatory machinery — sat assembled and dormant for tens of millions of years before the environment ever let it express. In AI the toolkit came together fast: attention, residual streams, layer norm, tokenization, and the pretraining objective were essentially all in place by 2018. The wait was for the ecology. It took roughly five years for the surroundings to turn permissive enough that the 2017 architecture could actually detonate, and the fuse burned through three distinct stages.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">The Long Fuse — AI's Ignition Sequence</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">Why the 2017 architecture didn't detonate until roughly five years later</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"cat-grid\">\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <span class=\"cat-n\">01 / 03</span>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">The Architecture</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">Vaswani et al. (2017) dispensed with recurrence and convolution. Self-attention — softmax(QK<sup>T</sup>/&radic;d<sub>k</sub>)V — lets every token attend to every other in O(n&sup2;) time, fully GPU-parallel across the sequence axis. Shipped, almost modestly, as a translation model.</div>\n      <span class=\"cat-stat\">2017 &middot; 8 authors &middot; &lt;12 pp</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <span class=\"cat-n\">02 / 03</span>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">The Scaling Laws</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">Kaplan et al. (2020) made the returns to scale quantitative; GPT-3 (Brown et al., 2020) revealed in-context learning emerging at 175B parameters; Chinchilla (Hoffmann et al., 2022, DeepMind) corrected Kaplan's ratio to roughly 20 training tokens per parameter. The detonation, arriving five years after the fuse was lit.</div>\n      <span class=\"cat-stat\">&asymp; 20 tokens : 1 param</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <span class=\"cat-n\">03 / 03</span>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">The Permissive Ecology</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">NVIDIA A100s, CommonCrawl at web scale, and three venture cycles of cheap capital turned the environment permissive. Bommasani et al. (2021) gave the result a name — foundation models — once there was enough of it to name.</div>\n      <span class=\"cat-stat\">compute = oxygen</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Section 0's ignition sequence, replayed in silicon: a toolkit finished by 2018, a scaling law made quantitative between 2020 and 2022, and an ecology that finally turned permissive. Sources: Vaswani et al. 2017; Kaplan et al. 2020; Brown et al. 2020; Hoffmann et al. 2022; Bommasani et al. 2021.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>In Section 0, oxygen was the catalyst that made large, active, predatory bodies metabolically affordable — the same role compute plays here. The architecture itself was, in some sense, going to arrive regardless: Bahdanau's 2015 work had already bolted attention onto a recurrent net, and stripping the recurrence away was the obvious next move for anyone watching closely. The genuinely contingent part was whether the surrounding environment would ever run hot enough for the architecture to matter. Between 2020 and 2022 it did, and a dormant design became the defining body plan of a new kingdom.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">What Went Extinct</h3>\n\n<p>Every real Cambrian has a second act that rarely makes the slide. In Section 0 I borrowed Bottjer's name for it — the Cambrian Substrate Revolution, the moment matgrounds became mixgrounds and the old way of living on the seafloor stopped being possible. The transformer boundary has the same second act. For the new body plan to spread across the field, an older world had to give way, and the more revealing part of the story is what quietly stopped being trained.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">After the Boundary</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">What the transformer made possible — and what it displaced</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"diptych\">\n    <div class=\"dip spec\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">What Emerged</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>Foundation models</strong> pretrained on &gt;10T tokens — one shared base that every downstream species now builds on.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>In-context learning</strong>, emergent at scale, first legible in GPT-3 (2020).</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>A single body plan</strong> — self-attention + residual streams + layer norm + a tokenizer — copied across every frontier lab.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"dip deci\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">What Fell Away</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>Everything RNN-shaped.</strong> By 2022, almost nobody was training a fresh LSTM for a serious language task.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>The handcrafted pipeline</strong> — POS tagging, dependency parsing, rule-based NER — still alive in production, no longer frontier.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>Expert systems.</strong> Cyc runs on as an intellectual curiosity rather than a competitor.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">The classical symbolic lineage survived three separate reversals across four decades, then lost its place at the frontier to transformer foundation models. Source: author's framing; Vaswani et al. 2017; Bommasani et al. 2021; Hoffmann et al. 2022.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>The symbolic tradition is the one that stays with me. It had outlasted the AI winter of the 1980s, the statistical-learning turn of the 2000s, and the deep-learning wave of the 2010s — written off three times, still standing each time. Transformer foundation models are what finally ended it, and they ended it the Ediacaran way. The world these systems were built for became a different place, and a way of working tuned to the old one had nowhere left to go.</p>\n\n<p>And then, the way it always seems to go in a Cambrian, the weird wonders came out.</p>\n\n<div class=\"end-orn\">&middot; &middot; &middot;</div>";

export const S3_HTML = "<p class=\"post-lede\">Gould said you could never predict which lineage would make it; Conway Morris said you always could. Settling their thirty-year fight is, oddly, the whole game for underwriting which AI companies survive the coming decimation.</p>\n\n<p class=\"drop-cap\">Stephen Jay Gould's <em>Wonderful Life</em> is a book about humility, mounted patiently over some 350 pages. Rewind the tape of life to the Burgess Shale, he argued — the thought experiment he called \"replaying life's tape\" — erase what happened, let it run again from an identical start, and the odds that anything resembling human intelligence shows up the second time are vanishingly small. The Cambrian, in his telling, was a lottery. Most of its winners were never pre-destined; they simply happened to persist.</p>\n\n<p>Simon Conway Morris disagreed. As one of Harry Whittington's students at Cambridge, he had spent the 1970s redescribing the Burgess fauna specimen by specimen, and he read the same fossils the other way. In <em>Life's Solution: Inevitable Humans in a Lonely Universe</em> (Cambridge, 2003) he argued that the forms life arrives at are pinned down by deep functional attractors. Replay the tape and you would get eyes, wings, jointed limbs, and intelligence all over again — maybe on a different chassis, but the same essential forms.</p>\n\n<p>Blount, Lenski, and Losos (2018), writing in <em>Science</em>, split the difference. Contingency dominates at large historical scales; convergence dominates at molecular and functional ones. Gould and Conway Morris had been arguing past each other on two different axes, and each was right on his own.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <div class=\"callout-tag\">The Payload for AI</div>\n  <p>This is roughly where AI architectures sit in 2026. Ask which single company owns the dominant consumer chat app in 2030 and you are deep in Gould territory — radically contingent, a coin-flip dressed up as a thesis. Ask what the winning architecture looks like and you are in Conway Morris territory — convergent, constrained, close to inevitable. Keep those two questions apart and most of the confusion about \"AI moats\" dissolves. Let me justify that.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">What Actually Survived the Burgess Shale</h3>\n\n<p>Look at what came through. The arthropod body plan — segmented body, external skeleton, jointed limbs — became, numerically, the body plan of animal life; arthropods are still roughly 80% of all described animal species on Earth. The chordate body plan — internal skeleton, notochord, pharyngeal slits, segmented muscles — gave us fish, then tetrapods, then us. Mollusks persist. Annelids and echinoderms persist alongside them.</p>\n\n<p>The weird anomalies did not. <em>Anomalocaris</em> left no descendants, and neither did <em>Opabinia</em>, <em>Hallucigenia</em>, or <em>Wiwaxia</em>. One of the largest macroscopic apex predators in the history of animal life — a meter-long body, compound eyes carrying some 16,000 lenses (Paterson et al., 2011) — vanished without a single heir. For anyone underwriting AI companies, that is a fact worth sitting with.</p>\n\n<p>Why did the arthropod and chordate plans persist while the wonders did not? Because their architectures <em>permitted scaling</em>. The arthropod exoskeleton delivered four things at once: protection against predation, a substrate for muscle attachment, sensory surface area, and desiccation resistance once the clade eventually invaded land. It paid real costs — molting leaves you briefly defenseless, and mineralized shells hit an upper size limit — but for most environments the benefits dominated. The chordate plan made the opposite bet, trading external armor for an internal structural axis, and that axis scaled magnificently. Whales reach 30 meters. No arthropod ever will.</p>\n\n<p>Here is the definition I'll lean on, because it does real work in this section: a <strong>vertebral axis</strong> is the internal, load-bearing spine an organism grows <em>along</em> — the support column that lets a body get larger and more capable without wrapping itself in an ever-heavier shell.</p>\n\n<p>Now map it onto AI. The exoskeleton's equivalent is the transformer — self-attention, residual streams, layer norm, a pretraining objective, a tokenizer. That stack is the arthropod body plan of AI, the shared chassis every frontier model is built on. And the vertebral axis? The <em>post-training stack</em>: the internal machinery that lets a model scale along a dimension raw pretraining never offered — test-time compute.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">Two Architectures That Scaled</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">Why the arthropod exoskeleton and the chordate vertebral axis both persisted — and their AI analogs.</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"diptych\">\n    <div class=\"dip spec\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">Exoskeleton · External</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>Biology.</strong> The arthropod plan — segmented body, external skeleton, jointed limbs. Numerically the winner of animal life: still ~80% of all described species.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>The cost.</strong> Molting leaves you defenseless; mineralized shells cap your size. There will never be a 30-meter arthropod.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>AI analog — the transformer.</strong> Self-attention + residual streams + layer norm + a pretraining objective + a tokenizer. The shared chassis beneath GPT, Claude, Gemini, Grok, and DeepSeek alike.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"dip deci\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">Vertebral Axis · Internal</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>Biology.</strong> The chordate plan — an internal structural axis (the notochord) in place of external armor. It scaled beautifully: whales reach 30 meters.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>The mechanism.</strong> The load-bearing spine a body grows <em>along</em>. Armor protects; an internal axis compounds.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\"><strong>AI analog — the post-training stack.</strong> RLHF (Ouyang et al., 2022), Constitutional AI (Bai et al., 2022), chain-of-thought (Wei et al., 2022), and test-time compute (o1/o3; DeepSeek-R1, 2025) — a new scaling axis: many tokens of deliberation instead of one forward pass.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\"><em>Both plans survived because both permitted scaling — one outward and armored, one inward and compounding.</em> Sources: Gould 1989; Conway Morris 2003; Ouyang et al. 2022 (InstructGPT); Bai et al. 2022 (Constitutional AI); Wei et al. 2022 (chain-of-thought). Biological-to-AI mapping is the author's framing.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>The post-training stack earns the \"internal axis\" label because of what it unlocked. Ouyang et al.'s 2022 InstructGPT paper established a three-stage recipe — supervised fine-tuning, reward-model training, then PPO optimization against that reward model — that every frontier lab now runs in some form. Bai et al. (2022) extended it with Anthropic's Constitutional AI, deriving part of the preference signal from the model critiquing its own outputs against a written constitution. Wei et al. (2022) introduced chain-of-thought as what looked, at first, like a mere prompting trick; OpenAI's o1 (September 2024) and o3 (December 2024) turned it into a first-class training paradigm, using RL to reward long internal reasoning traces, and DeepSeek-R1 (January 2025) reproduced the behavior in open weights at a fraction of the cost. The vertebrate move is the same one underneath: pay for capability with internal structure rather than external bulk.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <div class=\"callout-tag\">A Fair Technical Objection</div>\n  <p>One of my reviewers pushed back here, correctly: today's frontier models do <em>not</em> differ at the phylum level the way arthropods and chordates do. Architecturally, GPT, Claude, and Gemini are closer to one <em>genus</em> than one phylum — nearly the same body plan, not merely the same deep toolkit. He is right, and that convergence is itself the subject of a later section on carcinization. For now, read \"body plan\" as the shared transformer chassis rather than a claim that these systems have diverged into fundamentally different internal architectures — because they haven't, and that sameness is exactly the point.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">The Weird Wonders</h3>\n\n<p>So who are AI's weird wonders — the experiments that were genuinely strange, genuinely useful as data points, and genuinely never going to be the thing that persists? I have nominations. First-generation generic chatbots. Most character-AI and \"AI girlfriend\" apps. The bulk of the early-2023 GPT wrappers that slapped a system prompt on the API and sold a subscription. Early AutoGPT and BabyAGI, real Cambrian oddities every one. The first wave of orchestration frameworks — LangChain, LlamaIndex, CrewAI, Autogen, Swarm — radiating hard before anyone knew what an agent even was. (LangChain's own creator, Harrison Chase, essentially conceded the point in his 2025 reflection on three years of building the framework, acknowledging the abstraction bloat and pivoting to the lower-level LangGraph.) The three-second hallucinated \"AI video\" clips. The voice-clone startups. The \"AI agent that books your flight\" demos that turned out to be pre-scripted. Builder.ai, whose AI support system was reportedly closer to a room full of engineers doing the work by hand.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"pull\">\n  The fossil record files almost everything under substrate for whatever comes next, and the first-generation AI oddities are no exception — which is why their founders have earned the paleontologist's respect for building them at all.\n</blockquote>\n\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <div class=\"callout-tag\">The Hallucigenia Moment</div>\n  <p>Jasper hit a $1.5B valuation in October 2022 and quietly cut its own valuation inside a year — a shell that looked like a body plan until the substrate underneath it, the raw API, commoditized. That is the <em>Hallucigenia</em> moment: reconstructed with confidence, upside-down, then quietly corrected. And remember what happened to <em>Opabinia</em> — Whittington's 1975 redescription made the Palaeontological Association audibly laugh, and three decades later we understood it as a crucial stem-group arthropod that taught us how the whole anomalocaridid body plan got assembled. AutoGPT may read as a curiosity by 2026, yet it will be cited as the moment \"agentic\" crossed from science fiction into a product category.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">The Cognition-as-a-Service Stack, Biologically</h3>\n\n<p>Above the models sits the infrastructure, and I want to frame it explicitly using Mayfield's Cognition-as-a-Service (CaaS) taxonomy (Chaddha, 2024). Mayfield's picture is four tiers: cognitive plumbing (chips, compute, networking, power, cooling); models (foundation models, safety, monitoring); data and middleware (RAG, orchestration, observability); and applications (the actual digital co-workers). Laid over the Cambrian, the stack reads almost suspiciously cleanly — plumbing is the <em>ocean</em>, models are the <em>body plan</em>, middleware is the <em>organ system</em>, and applications are the <em>species</em>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">The CaaS Stack — Mapped to Cambrian Biology</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">Cognition-as-a-Service framework · Mayfield Fund · biological analogs are the author's framing.</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"funnel\">\n    <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#2C2522;border-radius:5px 5px 0 0;\"><span class=\"f-num\">1</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Cognitive Plumbing — The Ocean</div><div class=\"f-desc\">Chips, compute, networking, power, cooling. The substrate everything else swims in — and where the FLOPs (the oxygen) come from. NVIDIA: $215.9B FY26 revenue; $5T market cap crossed Oct 2025. When DeepSeek-R1 hinted the ocean might be smaller than priced, NVIDIA shed ~$589B in a single day (Jan 2025) — the whole stack is that leveraged to the substrate.</div><div class=\"f-bio\">The primordial sea — the substrate every layer above depends on.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">NVIDIA</span><span class=\"fc\">AMD MI300</span><span class=\"fc\">TSMC</span><span class=\"fc\">Google TPU</span><span class=\"fc\">Groq</span></div></div></div>\n    <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#3D2E20;margin:0 12px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">2</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Foundation Models — The Body Plans</div><div class=\"f-desc\">Pre-trained on trillions of tokens. Transformer = the arthropod exoskeleton; post-training stack = the vertebral axis. Differences in philosophy matter at the species level; the body plan is shared.</div><div class=\"f-bio\">Body plans. Everything downstream is built on one of these.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">OpenAI</span><span class=\"fc\">Anthropic</span><span class=\"fc\">Google DeepMind</span><span class=\"fc\">DeepSeek</span><span class=\"fc\">Meta Llama</span></div></div></div>\n    <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#5A3D22;margin:0 24px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">3</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Data &amp; Middleware — The Organ Systems</div><div class=\"f-desc\">MCP connects models to tools and data (an agent to its environment) — the circulatory system. A2A connects agents to each other (one organism to its conspecifics) — the nervous system. Both are being pushed toward open, multi-stakeholder governance rather than any single vendor's control.</div><div class=\"f-bio\">Organ systems. MCP = circulatory. A2A = nervous.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">Anthropic MCP</span><span class=\"fc\">Google A2A</span><span class=\"fc\">LangGraph</span><span class=\"fc\">LlamaIndex</span><span class=\"fc\">Pinecone</span></div></div></div>\n    <div class=\"f-layer\" style=\"background:#4f74ad;margin:0 36px;border-radius:0 0 5px 5px;\"><span class=\"f-num\">4</span><div class=\"f-body\"><div class=\"f-title\">Applications — The Species</div><div class=\"f-desc\">The vertical and horizontal products adapting to specific niches. Some will turn out to be <em>Trilobites</em> (270-million-year dynasties), some whales, some <em>Anomalocaris</em> (apex, then extinct).</div><div class=\"f-bio\">Species. The layer where a durable spine can — or fails to — form.</div><div class=\"fchips\"><span class=\"fc\">Cursor</span><span class=\"fc\">Perplexity</span><span class=\"fc\">Harvey</span><span class=\"fc\">Glean</span><span class=\"fc\">Sierra</span><span class=\"fc\">Abridge</span></div></div></div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\"><em>NVIDIA is the ocean. The labs are the body plans. MCP + A2A are the organ systems. The applications are the species.</em> Framework: Navin Chaddha, Mayfield Fund — \"Cognition-as-a-Service\" (2024). Company examples illustrative; biological analogs are the author's framing.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>MCP (Anthropic, November 2024) and A2A (Google, April 2025) are the organ systems — MCP wiring a model to its environment, A2A wiring agents to one another. The direction of travel is toward open standards rather than private moats: Google handed A2A to the Linux Foundation in 2025, and MCP has moved onto multi-stakeholder governance rather than staying under any single lab's control. Once the plumbing between agents becomes a public protocol, it stops being anyone's defensible position — which brings us to the question a good reviewer is already shouting at the page.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Exoskeleton or Vertebra? The Moat Question</h3>\n\n<p>For any given company, is its structure an <em>exoskeleton</em> — protective, real, but a shared shell that everyone molts into and that eventually commoditizes — or a <em>vertebral axis</em>, a durable internal spine a single company can actually defend and grow along? That is the question that decides whether you are underwriting a whale or an <em>Anomalocaris</em>. My verdict, layer by layer:</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">Exoskeleton or Vertebra? — A Moat Verdict</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">For each tier of the stack: can a single company defend the structure, or is it shared substrate everyone grows the same way?</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"vgrid\">\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Semis · NVIDIA</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">The Ocean</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">The water the whole stack swims in. A near-monopoly moat today, though a substrate everything is leveraged to rather than a defensible species. Its risk is the ocean itself shrinking — as the ~$589B single-day wipeout after DeepSeek-R1 showed.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Foundation Models</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">Exoskeleton</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">A shared chassis everyone molts into. Necessary, though rarely a per-company moat — the frontier is carcinizing toward one shape. The live risk is becoming an airline: high fixed cost, low marginal cost, prices competed toward the floor.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">MCP + A2A</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">Organs → Open</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">The organ systems are standardizing into open protocols under multi-stakeholder governance. Excellent for the ecosystem; by design, nothing any single company can wall off.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Applications</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">Vertebra?</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">The one tier where a durable internal axis can form — proprietary data flywheels, workflow lock-in, distribution. A real moat if the company grows a spine; otherwise a GPT-wrapper that molts once and dies.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\"><em>The moat lives at the ends of the stack, not the middle.</em> The shared chassis (models) and the public protocols (middleware) commoditize by design; durable defensibility concentrates in the ocean below and the species above. Author's framing.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>So \"is this a moat?\" turns out to be a question about position more than technology. Where a structure sits in the stack decides almost everything about whether one company can defend it. Durable advantage comes from the vertebral axis: the internal, compounding structure that lets one organism keep scaling while the shared shell stays shared. In AI that means a proprietary post-training flywheel at the model layer and a proprietary data-and-workflow flywheel at the application layer. Everything between them hardens into ocean and organs — universal, necessary, and worth nothing as a defensible position.</p>\n\n<p>Which leaves the hardest question. Some of these species will be whales, some <em>Trilobites</em>, and some <em>Anomalocaris</em> — apex for an age, then gone without descendants. Sorting them is a question about the food chain, about who eats whom, and that is where the apex predators come in.</p>\n\n<div class=\"end-orn\">· · ·</div>";

export const S4_HTML = "<p class=\"lead drop-cap\">Robert Paine pried a starfish off a rock in Mukkaw Bay, Washington, in 1963, with a crowbar. He kept doing it — clearing <em>Pisaster ochraceus</em> off one stretch of tide pool and leaving an identical stretch alone as a control. Within a year the pool he had \"cleaned\" collapsed from fifteen species to eight, as <em>Mytilus</em> mussels monopolized the rock and crowded everything else out.</p>\n\n<p>Paine published that experiment in <em>The American Naturalist</em> in 1966 — \"Food Web Complexity and Species Diversity\" — and three years later gave the effect a name he borrowed from architecture: the <strong>keystone species</strong>, the stone at the crown of an arch that holds every other stone in place. Pull the keystone and the arch comes down.</p>\n\n<p>The insight has since been replicated in nearly every ecosystem anyone has bothered to poke. Sea otters structure kelp forests by eating urchins. Wolves reintroduced to Yellowstone restructured river geomorphology by suppressing elk. Sharks organize reef ecology from the top down. Estes and twenty-three co-authors, in the 2011 <em>Science</em> review \"Trophic Downgrading of Planet Earth,\" called the loss of apex consumers \"humankind's most pervasive influence on nature.\" Take the top out of a food web and the whole thing reorganizes around the hole.</p>\n\n<p>Now apply that to AI.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Who Runs the Reef</h3>\n\n<p>Who is the apex predator of the AI ecosystem in 2026? A handful of frontier labs, plus NVIDIA, plus the hyperscalers that bankroll them. Unlike a reef, this food chain files paperwork, so we can price it.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">The Food Chain, Priced</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">The apex orgs of the 2026 AI ecosystem — last-reported private marks and capex</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"vgrid\">\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">OpenAI</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$500B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">Secondary, Oct 2025 — $157B (Oct '24) → $300B (Apr '25) → $500B, with IPO talk toward $1T. First-half '25 revenue ~$4.3B against projected losses of $17B in 2026 widening to $47B by 2028 (Barclays). Eats the most capital, by a wide margin.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Anthropic</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$380B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">Series G, Feb 12 2026 — $30B raised, six-times oversubscribed, the second-largest private tech round in history. Run-rate $1B (early '25) → a targeted ~$9B. 40% of enterprise AI spend and 54% of the coding market (Menlo Ventures). The rising threat to OpenAI on enterprise footing.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Google DeepMind</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$91–93B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">Alphabet capex, 2025 — up to $250B planned by 2027. Gemini 3 Pro (Nov '25) and 3.1 Pro (Feb '26) each briefly topped LMArena. Structurally the best-positioned apex: it owns its own distribution — Search, Chrome, Android — and ships the second-largest inference revenue.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">xAI</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$1.25T</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">SpaceX all-stock combined entity, Feb 2026 — $80B (Mar '25) → $230B Series E (Jan '26) → merger. ~$3.8B combined ARR. Memphis \"Colossus\" runs 200–230K H100/H200s and targets 1M GPUs — the single largest known training cluster on Earth.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Meta</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$70–72B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">2025 capex — carrying the open-weight Llama family as a strategic loss-leader. Caught submitting a customized Maverick variant to LMArena rather than the shipped weights in April 2025, a tell about how much the leaderboard now matters.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\" style=\"border-top-color:var(--fig-warm);\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">DeepSeek</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">&gt;$10B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">Targeted first external raise, early 2026 — the Chinese apex. V3 trained for a reported $5.576M (Dec '24); R1 (Jan '25) matched o1-level reasoning in open weights. Arguably the single most disruptive lab of 2024–25, and the textbook case of ecological release.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Sources: primary rounds &amp; secondaries 2024–2026; Alphabet and Meta capex guidance; DeepSeek-V3 Technical Report (arXiv:2412.19437). Valuations are last-reported private marks — several are secondary or forward-looking. All six sit on NVIDIA, the substrate none of them own.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>Below the apex swim the mid-trophic labs — Mistral, Cohere, SSI, Alibaba's Qwen, Moonshot's Kimi, Thinking Machines, Poolside, Magic. A crowded band. Radiations like this get pruned hard; the fossil record is emphatic that most of a burst does not survive its own explosion. Some of these will persist as specialists. History suggests the majority will not.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Apex or Keystone?</h3>\n\n<p>Here I owe a correction, because a sharp reader caught me smuggling two different ideas under one word. I opened with Paine's <em>keystone</em> — and then spent a whole food chain calling everyone an <em>apex predator</em>. Those are two different jobs, and the gap between them is the entire point of this section.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">Apex ≠ Keystone</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">Two ecological ideas the ecosystem keeps conflating</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"diptych\">\n    <div class=\"dip deci\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">Apex Predator</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Defined by <strong>position and consumption</strong>: the largest thing at the top of the chain, the one that eats the most.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Remove it and a rival usually slides up into the vacancy, and total diversity stays roughly flat. The reef gets a new landlord.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">In AI: whoever is burning the most capital and compute this quarter. A title that changes hands.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"dip spec\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">Keystone Species</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Defined by <strong>structuring effect out of all proportion to its biomass</strong> (Paine, 1969).</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Remove it and the whole community collapses, because the keystone was the one thing suppressing a monopolist. Paine's starfish kept the mussels in check; pull it and they take the rock.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">In AI: the player whose removal would let a single body plan or standard exclude everyone else.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Robert Paine's <em>Pisaster</em> was both the biggest predator on the rock and the keystone whose removal dropped the pool from 15 species to 8 (Paine 1966, 1969). In AI the two roles rarely sit inside the same organism.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>That distinction settles a fair objection: delete a pure apex predator and the species count usually holds steady, since a rival just takes its place. The count collapses only when the vanished predator was holding back a second, more destructive force. In the tide pool that force is the mussels. In AI it is a single body plan or standard poised to pave over everyone else. Suppressing that monopolist is what earns a keystone its name.</p>\n\n<p>So which of our giants is merely apex, and which is keystone? Most of the labs are apex. Swap OpenAI and Anthropic in the ranking and the ecosystem barely notices; a rival slides up. NVIDIA is the keystone, and it isn't close. When the DeepSeek-R1 release on January 27, 2025 briefly suggested the substrate might be smaller than everyone had priced in, NVIDIA shed roughly $589 billion of market value in a single day — the largest one-day loss in U.S. history. The market was repricing the entire arch off one stone.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">What Makes a Keystone Player</h3>\n\n<p>If size is not the test, what is? Pull the common traits out of the food chain above and the players that structure the ecosystem — hyperscalers, the two or three closest labs, NVIDIA — share four characteristics. Bigness on its own is none of them.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">What Makes a Keystone Player</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">The common traits, recapped — the test is structuring effect, not size</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"cat-grid\">\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <span class=\"cat-n\">01 / 04</span>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Control of a Scarce Substrate</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">Whoever gates the resource everything else runs on — NVIDIA's FLOPs, Alphabet's balance sheet, the hyperscalers' distribution channels — sets the terms for everyone downstream. You cannot compete for a niche you can be locked out of at the supply line.</div>\n      <span class=\"cat-stat\">$675B+ hyperscaler capex, 2026</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <span class=\"cat-n\">02 / 04</span>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Price- and Standard-Setting</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">These players define the market's floor: per-token pricing, the benchmark bar everyone else has to clear, the protocols the ecosystem standardizes on (MCP, A2A). Move one of those numbers and everyone downstream re-plans around it.</div>\n      <span class=\"cat-stat\">Sets the market's price floor</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <span class=\"cat-n\">03 / 04</span>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Capital Gravity</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">A round large enough to bend the funding landscape starves rivals before they can radiate. A $30B, six-times-oversubscribed raise walls off a niche at a price most challengers will never reach the substrate to match.</div>\n      <span class=\"cat-stat\">$30B in a single round</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <span class=\"cat-n\">04 / 04</span>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Suppression of a Monopolist</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">The genuinely keystone trait: keeping any single body plan or standard from shutting out all the others. While three or four apex labs check one another, the ecosystem stays diverse. Remove one, let another take the whole niche, and it simplifies.</div>\n      <span class=\"cat-stat\">3–4 labs in balance</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Author's framing, built on Paine's keystone criteria. The operative question is whose removal would let one competitor take the whole substrate.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <div class=\"callout-tag\">Is the Apex Even Profitable?</div>\n  <p>No, and the reason is more interesting than the valuations on the grid above. None of these predators turns a corporate profit in 2026. The losses on the OpenAI and Anthropic cards are not teething costs a bigger quarter erases; they are the steady-state metabolism of an apex organism, and most credible forecasts put break-even no earlier than the back half of the decade.</p>\n  <p>Ecology explains why. Apex predators carry the highest per-capita energy demand in any system; they are the largest and the widest-ranging, and they starve first when the substrate contracts. Being apex is an expense the ecosystem pays, and the cheap capital that covers it can leave. That is what makes the removal question sharp: these are costly organisms living on money that is not promised to them.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Ecological Release</h3>\n\n<p>Which brings us to DeepSeek, and to a move ecologists watch play out again and again. When a dominant predator withdraws from a niche — turns its attention elsewhere, in this case toward monetizing its lead — the pressure it was exerting lifts, and something smaller, faster, and hungrier expands into the vacated space. Ecologists call it <strong>ecological release</strong> (or competitive release), and DeepSeek is a textbook specimen. As the Western apex leaned into premium products and enterprise contracts, DeepSeek trained V3 for a reported $5.576M and shipped R1 in open weights at o1-level reasoning, colonizing the low-cost, open-frontier niche the incumbents had quietly abandoned.</p>\n\n<p>And it is not alone in that niche. The open-weight band is filling from several directions at once — Meta's Llama family anchoring it in the West, alongside a still-emerging cohort of Western open-model labs and Alibaba's Qwen and Moonshot's Kimi in the East. Writing off the open-weight radiation as \"just China\" mistakes a single successful colonizer for the whole niche. Release events attract more than one opportunist.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">One Trophic Level Down</h3>\n\n<p>Below the apex sits the application layer — the organisms that feed on the models rather than build them. A few are worth staring at, because their growth curves are not normal.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">One Trophic Level Down</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">The application layer — valuations and ARR one level below the model layer</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"vgrid\">\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Cursor · Anysphere</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$29.3B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">Nov 2025 — $400M (Aug '24) → $29.3B, with talks at $50–60B by April 2026. ARR went from essentially zero to over $2B in roughly three years: by a wide margin the fastest B2B scaling in history.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Sierra</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$10B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">Bret Taylor's AI customer-service company hit $100M ARR in 21 months — a roughly 100× revenue multiple at its valuation. A specialist feeding on a single, defensible workflow.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Harvey</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$11B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">Legal AI — $3B → $11B in 13 months, on $195M ARR at the end of 2025. Proprietary workflow and domain data carry the moat, well ahead of the underlying model.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"vc\">\n      <div class=\"vname\">Perplexity</div>\n      <div class=\"vspend\">$20B</div>\n      <div class=\"vcos\">~$500M annualized by April 2026. An answer engine layered over models it does not train; a clean test of whether the application layer can hold a moat.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Sources: reported private valuations and ARR disclosures, 2024–2026. All figures are last-reported private marks and move monthly. This layer sits one trophic level below the model layer and feeds directly on it.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>The old objection to every company on that grid was that they are \"GPT wrappers\" — thin UI over somebody else's model, destined to be eaten by that somebody else the moment the model-maker ships the same feature. That was the dominant thesis from late 2022 through mid-2024. The counter-argument came from investors who had watched the pattern before. <strong>Andrew Chen</strong>, a general partner at a16z's Speedrun program, wrote \"Revenge of the GPT Wrappers\" in February 2025; <strong>Martin Casado</strong> and <strong>Sarah Wang</strong> of a16z followed with \"Where Value Will Accrue in AI\" in May 2025; and <strong>Sarah Guo</strong> of Conviction has been making the same case since 2023. Their argument: enterprise distribution, proprietary data, workflow integration, and domain-specific plumbing are the new moats, and the \"wrapper\" pejorative is a category error.</p>\n\n<p>Looking at the 2024–2026 data, I think both camps are right at different scales. Generic wrappers with no proprietary workflow or data (first-wave Jasper, first-wave AI voice, most generic chat apps) are being decimated exactly as predicted. Cursor-class wrappers — deep product-UX moats, tight model integration, relentless dogfooding, a specific user segment — are turning into real companies. The dot-com vintage is the honest analogy: Pets.com and Webvan died because their unit economics were a bubble artifact; Amazon and Google survived the same bubble because their unit economics, revealed late, were genuinely excellent. A tournament with many contestants, and the survivors are the ones who figured out the substrate.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">The Airline Endgame</h3>\n\n<p><strong>David Cahn</strong> at Sequoia wrote the essential bubble-alarm essays of this cycle — \"AI's $200B Question\" (September 2023), then \"AI's $600B Question\" (June 2024). His arithmetic is brutal and simple: take NVIDIA's data-center revenue, double it for full infrastructure cost, double it again for a 50% end-user gross margin, and you get the AI revenue the ecosystem must eventually generate to justify current capex. In 2023 that implied roughly $200B of revenue against something far smaller actually landing. A year later the gap was $600B. But it is a single sentence of his, almost thrown away, that I keep returning to.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"pull\">\n  \"Without a monopoly or oligopoly, high fixed cost + low marginal cost businesses almost always see prices competed down to marginal cost (e.g., airlines).\"\n  <cite>— David Cahn, Sequoia Capital</cite>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>That is the paleobiological claim restated in the language of economics. Airlines are the trilobites of the 20th-century industrial ecosystem: once dominant, now stuck in a permanent red ocean of razor-thin margins and periodic bankruptcy. If foundation models commoditize — if the crab-shape of the frontier model grows so convergent that everybody's Claude, GPT, Gemini, Grok, and DeepSeek look and perform roughly the same at roughly the same price — then the model layer becomes an airline industry. The real margin then accrues elsewhere: <em>upstream</em>, to NVIDIA, TSMC, and ASML (the Dutch lithography monopoly put about €1.3B <em>into</em> Mistral for a ~11% stake in September 2025, becoming its top shareholder — a sign of how badly the deepest upstream player wants a foothold on the way down), and <em>downstream</em>, to the applications with genuine proprietary-data moats.</p>\n\n<p>The framework I hold this against is <strong>Carlota Perez</strong>'s <em>Technological Revolutions and Financial Capital</em> (2002). Her model runs in two acts — Installation (Irruption → Frenzy → Turning Point → Crash) and Deployment (Synergy → Maturity) — each roughly fifty to sixty years, with a financial crisis sitting at the hinge. The dot-com boom-bust was the hinge of the Information &amp; Telecommunications revolution. Perez argues that bubbles are constitutive of technological revolutions, the mechanism by which capital gets deployed rather than a pathology to be avoided. I think we are somewhere in the late Frenzy of a revolution best called \"cognition,\" and the turning point has not happened yet. The January 2025 DeepSeek shock was only a tremor. The quake is the one that ends the easy-money regime for any model company not within a factor of two of the frontier.</p>\n\n<p>Who is the apex predator of the AI ecosystem? Right now: the hyperscalers, the three or four labs closest to them, and NVIDIA beneath them all as the keystone. In 2030? Not necessarily. <em>Anomalocaris</em>, with its meter-long body and sixteen thousand lenses per eye, also had no competitors. It ruled the Cambrian seas as the first great apex predator in the history of animal life. And then it left no descendants at all.</p>\n\n<div class=\"end-orn\">· · ·</div>";

export const S5_HTML = "<p class=\"lead drop-cap\">This is the section that will earn me the most emails from biology professors — and it is the one I am most sure of. Line up every frontier model shipped between 2024 and 2026 the way a taxonomist lines up a drawer of pinned specimens. Rivals on the labels; the same animal in the drawer. Independently — from different founders, different compute clusters, different countries, different philosophical priors — the labs have all grown the same body. They have all become crabs.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"stat-grid\">\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">&ge;5</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">independent decapod lineages that evolved the crab form — plus horseshoe crabs, from outside Crustacea entirely (Scholtz 2014; Wolfe et al. 2021)</span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">40–65&times;</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">times image-forming eyes evolved independently across the tree of life</span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">&lt;9 pts</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">spread between the top frontier models on SWE-bench Verified, late 2025</span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"stat-box\">\n        <span class=\"stat-val\">8+</span>\n        <span class=\"stat-label\">architectural traits every current flagship model now ships in common</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>Carcinization is a real phenomenon, and a strange one. The term was coined by L. A. Borradaile in 1916 to describe \"the evolutionary tendency of crustaceans to evolve a crab-like form.\" Gerhard Scholtz's 2014 paper in <em>Contributions to Zoology</em> — \"Evolution of crabs: history and deconstruction of a prime example of convergence\" — is the foundational modern treatment, and Jo Wolfe, Javier Luque, and Heather Bracken-Grissom sharpened it in their 2021 <em>BioEssays</em> paper, \"How to become a crab: Phenotypic constraints on a recurring body plan.\" What they show is almost comic: at least five separate lineages within Decapoda have arrived at crab-shape more or less on their own, and evolution keeps re-running the experiment.</p>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">How to Become a Crab — Five Roads, and One From Outside</h3>\n\n    <p>True crabs (Brachyura, who managed it at least twice within their own group), king crabs (Lithodidae, descended from hermit-crab ancestors — see Tsang et al., 2011, \"Hermit to King\"), porcelain crabs (Porcellanidae), the hairy stone crab (Lomisidae), and slipper lobsters (Scyllaridae) all converged on the same silhouette. That is five decapod lineages. Then there are horseshoe crabs — chelicerates, closer kin to spiders than to anything in that drawer — which reached a crab-like shape from a still further remove. Five roads inside the crustaceans, one from outside them: six independent routes to a single body.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">Carcinization — Six Roads to the Same Body</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Independent lineages that converged on the crab form · SVG reconstructions</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"creature-grid\">\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">True crabs</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Brachyura <span class=\"p\">|</span> The template</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M60 30 Q44 22 32 28\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M62 34 Q44 32 32 38\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M62 38 Q46 42 38 48\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M130 30 Q146 22 158 28\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M128 34 Q146 32 158 38\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M128 38 Q144 42 152 48\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M64 26 Q52 16 40 20 Q49 22 47 29 Z\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n            <path d=\"M126 26 Q138 16 150 20 Q141 22 143 29 Z\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"32\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" opacity=\"0.9\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"29\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#345583\" opacity=\"0.3\"/>\n            <line x1=\"87\" y1=\"18\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n            <line x1=\"103\" y1=\"18\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"85\" cy=\"9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"105\" cy=\"9\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Flattened carapace · widened sternum · abdomen folded under · <strong>evolved crab-form &ge;2&times; within Brachyura alone</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">~7,000 species <span class=\"p\">|</span> The reference body plan</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">King crabs</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Lithodidae <span class=\"p\">|</span> Ex-hermit crabs</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M60 30 Q44 22 30 26\" stroke=\"#b3894f\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M62 34 Q44 34 30 40\" stroke=\"#b3894f\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M62 38 Q46 44 40 50\" stroke=\"#b3894f\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M130 30 Q148 24 160 30\" stroke=\"#b3894f\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M128 35 Q146 40 156 46\" stroke=\"#b3894f\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M64 26 Q52 16 40 20 Q49 22 47 29 Z\" fill=\"#b3894f\"/>\n            <path d=\"M126 26 Q138 15 151 19 Q142 22 144 29 Z\" fill=\"#b3894f\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"32\" rx=\"32\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#b3894f\" opacity=\"0.9\"/>\n            <path d=\"M72 20 l3 -6 3 6 M88 17 l3 -7 3 7 M104 17 l3 -7 3 7 M118 20 l3 -6 3 6\" stroke=\"#7a5a2a\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" fill=\"none\"/>\n            <line x1=\"87\" y1=\"19\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"11\" stroke=\"#7a5a2a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n            <line x1=\"103\" y1=\"19\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"11\" stroke=\"#7a5a2a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"85\" cy=\"10\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#7a5a2a\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"105\" cy=\"10\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#7a5a2a\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Descended from asymmetric hermit-crab ancestors · reduced last leg pair · spiny carapace · <strong>\"Hermit to King,\" Tsang et al. 2011</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Up to ~1.8 m leg-span <span class=\"p\">|</span> Cold, deep water</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Porcelain crabs</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Porcellanidae <span class=\"p\">|</span> Not true crabs</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M66 30 Q52 24 42 28\" stroke=\"#89a7cc\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M66 34 Q52 34 44 40\" stroke=\"#89a7cc\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M124 30 Q138 24 148 28\" stroke=\"#89a7cc\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M124 34 Q138 34 146 40\" stroke=\"#89a7cc\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M70 26 Q60 18 50 21 Q57 23 56 29 Z\" fill=\"#89a7cc\"/>\n            <path d=\"M120 26 Q130 18 140 21 Q133 23 134 29 Z\" fill=\"#89a7cc\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"32\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#89a7cc\" opacity=\"0.9\"/>\n            <path d=\"M88 20 Q80 6 66 4\" stroke=\"#5a7ba8\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" fill=\"none\"/>\n            <path d=\"M102 20 Q110 6 124 4\" stroke=\"#5a7ba8\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" fill=\"none\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"88\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"102\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Actually closer to squat lobsters · long antennae · flattened crab body arrived independently · filter-feeding</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">&lt;2 cm typical <span class=\"p\">|</span> Convergent, not related</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Hairy stone crab</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Lomisidae <span class=\"p\">|</span> A lineage of one</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M62 30 Q46 22 34 28\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M62 35 Q46 35 34 41\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M128 30 Q144 22 156 28\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M128 35 Q144 35 156 41\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M64 26 Q52 16 41 20 Q50 22 48 29 Z\" fill=\"#b3a48a\"/>\n            <path d=\"M126 26 Q138 16 149 20 Q140 22 142 29 Z\" fill=\"#b3a48a\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"32\" rx=\"32\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#b3a48a\" opacity=\"0.92\"/>\n            <g stroke=\"#8a7a5a\" stroke-width=\"1\" opacity=\"0.7\">\n              <line x1=\"78\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"76\" y2=\"18\"/><line x1=\"86\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"15\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"21\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"14\"/><line x1=\"104\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"15\"/><line x1=\"112\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"114\" y2=\"18\"/>\n              <line x1=\"82\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"40\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"42\"/><line x1=\"108\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"40\"/>\n            </g>\n            <line x1=\"88\" y1=\"19\" x2=\"86\" y2=\"12\" stroke=\"#8a7a5a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n            <line x1=\"102\" y1=\"19\" x2=\"104\" y2=\"12\" stroke=\"#8a7a5a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"86\" cy=\"11\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#8a7a5a\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"104\" cy=\"11\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#8a7a5a\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Sole member of its family · dense covering of setae (\"hair\") · tucks abdomen like a true crab · southern Australian rocky shores</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">~2 cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> An evolutionary singleton</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Slipper lobsters</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Scyllaridae <span class=\"p\">|</span> Halfway crab</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M60 32 Q44 28 34 32\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M60 36 Q44 36 36 42\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M130 32 Q146 28 156 32\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <path d=\"M130 36 Q146 36 154 42\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <rect x=\"58\" y=\"30\" width=\"74\" height=\"16\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#345583\" opacity=\"0.9\"/>\n            <ellipse cx=\"95\" cy=\"24\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#345583\" opacity=\"0.55\"/>\n            <path d=\"M70 22 Q66 12 78 10 L86 18 Z\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n            <path d=\"M120 22 Q124 12 112 10 L104 18 Z\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"88\" cy=\"30\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#89a7cc\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"102\" cy=\"30\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#89a7cc\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Broad flattened antennae like shovels · compact shortened body · partway down the same road, from a lobster start</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Up to ~50 cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> Bottom-dwelling</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\" style=\"border-top-color:var(--charcoal);\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Horseshoe crab</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\" style=\"color:var(--mid);\">Xiphosura <span class=\"p\">|</span> Not even a crustacean</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M62 40 Q62 16 95 15 Q128 16 128 40 Z\" fill=\"#6e7a4a\" opacity=\"0.9\"/>\n            <path d=\"M78 40 Q78 24 95 23 Q112 24 112 40 Z\" fill=\"#566036\" opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n            <line x1=\"128\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"176\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#566036\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"84\" cy=\"26\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#3e4626\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"106\" cy=\"26\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#3e4626\"/>\n            <path d=\"M70 40 l-4 8 M84 41 l-3 9 M106 41 l3 9 M120 40 l4 8\" stroke=\"#6e7a4a\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">A chelicerate — closer to spiders than to crabs · domed carapace + long tail telson · <strong>convergent from the furthest remove of all</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Up to ~60 cm <span class=\"p\">|</span> Lineage ~445 My old</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Sources: Scholtz 2014, <em>Contributions to Zoology</em>; Wolfe, Luque &amp; Bracken-Grissom 2021, <em>BioEssays</em>; Tsang et al. 2011. At least five lineages within Decapoda evolved the crab form independently; horseshoe crabs converge on the same shape from outside the crustaceans entirely — six roads, one body.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>The recipe is three coupled modifications: the carapace flattens and shortens, the sternum widens, and the pleon — the abdomen — reduces and folds up under the body. What you end up with is compact, armored, laterally mobile, and defensively tucked when threatened, and natural selection evidently likes it across a startling range of environments. So non-crabs keep turning into crabs. And because evolution has no destination, a few Brachyuran lineages have run the other way through <em>decarcinization</em> and stretched back out into long-bodied forms. (Both \"decarcinization\" and \"carcinizing\" are terms of art in this literature, in case you were reaching for the reply button.)</p>\n\n    <p>Simon Conway Morris would read all of this as vindication. His claim, mounted across <em>Life's Solution</em>, is that the space of viable body plans has attractors — Mount-Fuji-shaped basins of selective advantage — and that any sufficiently long evolutionary process eventually slides into one. Eyes are his favorite exhibit. Dan-Eric Nilsson and Susanne Pelger's 1994 calculation put the evolution of a camera-type eye from a flat photosensitive patch at roughly 364,000 generations — a geological blink — and eyes have duly appeared independently somewhere between 40 and 65 times. C4 photosynthesis: 60-plus independent origins. Wings: four, in insects, pterosaurs, birds, and bats. The fusiform, streamlined body of the fast swimmer: four more shots at one blueprint, in sharks, ichthyosaurs, dolphins, and tuna, from four unrelated ancestries.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">Convergence Scoreboard</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">How many times key body plans re-evolved independently across the tree of life</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Image-forming eyes</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>40–65 times</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">C4 photosynthesis</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:95%;background:var(--blue-mid)\"><span>60+ times</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Crab body form</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\" style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:9%;background:var(--fig-warm)\"></div>\n            <span class=\"bc-zero\">&ge;5 times</span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Wings</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\" style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:7%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"></div>\n            <span class=\"bc-zero\">4 times</span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Fusiform body</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\" style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:7%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"></div>\n            <span class=\"bc-zero\">4 times</span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Sources: Nilsson &amp; Pelger 1994; Land &amp; Nilsson on eye origins; Sage 2004 on C4; standard convergent-evolution literature. Counts are order-of-magnitude scholarly estimates, scaled here to the largest. Whatever the exact tallies, the pattern holds — nature keeps walking into the same forms.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Now Look at the Models</h3>\n\n    <p>Now open the other drawer — every flagship a frontier lab shipped between 2024 and 2026. Every one has: a transformer core, increasingly sparse (DeepSeek V3's 671B-total / 37B-active Mixture-of-Experts, Mixtral's 8&times;7B, Llama 4 Scout's 17B-active); pretraining on north of 10 trillion tokens; a post-training stack of supervised fine-tuning plus preference learning (RLHF or RLAIF), often with a constitutional layer on top; an explicit chain-of-thought or reasoning mode, RL-optimized against verifiable rewards (o1/o3, DeepSeek R1, Claude's thinking mode, Gemini Deep Think); tool and function calling, increasingly over MCP; multimodal ingestion — text and vision at a minimum, frequently audio; long context, 200K at baseline, 1M at the frontier, a headline 10M for Llama 4 Scout; a computer-use mode or an agent harness; and a matched pair of variants — one reasoning-heavy, one cheap — sharing backbone components.</p>\n\n    <p>That is the crab. Claude 4.5 Sonnet, GPT-5.2, Gemini 3.1 Pro, Grok 3, DeepSeek V3.2, Llama 4 Maverick, Qwen3, Mistral Large 2 — every one of them ships that same body plan. The differentiators have moved off the architecture and onto doctrine.</p>\n\n    <p>Anthropic ships Constitutional AI (Bai et al., 2022), explicit character training, and a Responsible Scaling Policy with graded AI Safety Levels — Claude's personality treated as an actual product asset. OpenAI leans on visible reasoning traces, the unified GPT-5 router, and deep Azure distribution. Google DeepMind presses its structural edges: native 1M–2M-token context, native multimodality, Deep Think, and delivery straight through Workspace and Search. DeepSeek, Mistral, and Llama 4 compete on Mixture-of-Experts aggression and open weights. Meta's differentiator is a license — an open-weight community grant with a 700-million-monthly-active-user carve-out, which is itself a rather clever piece of body-plan design.</p>\n\n    <p>And the benchmarks bunch up within single-digit points of one another.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">The Frontier Crab, Measured</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">SWE-bench Verified, late 2025 · The flagships cluster within single-digit points</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Claude Opus 4.5</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:80.9%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>80.9% (Nov 2025)</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">Gemini 3 Pro + Live-SWE</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:77.4%;background:var(--blue-mid)\"><span>77.4%</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"bc-row\">\n          <span class=\"bc-label\">GPT-5.2</span>\n          <div class=\"bc-track\">\n            <div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:77%;background:var(--blue-light)\"><span>~77% (same zone)</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Author's forward-dated figures (mid-2026 vantage), per lab reports. Beyond coding: on HLE (Phan et al. 2025) frontier reasoning models all cleared 50% in 2025; on ARC-AGI-1, OpenAI's o3 hit 87.5% in Dec 2024 — the first cross of the human threshold, though at ~$6,677 per task in high-compute mode (Chollet 2024); on ARC-AGI-2, Gemini 3.1 Pro topped ~77% in early 2026, with the field converging behind it.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <blockquote class=\"pull\">\n      This is textbook carcinization at the model layer — eight flagships, one body plan, each of them arrived at along a separate road.\n    </blockquote>\n\n    <h3 class=\"sub\">Gould, Conway Morris, and the Tape of Life</h3>\n\n    <p>There are two honest ways to read this, and they are the same two readings paleontologists have been arguing about for forty years. Stephen Jay Gould's <em>Wonderful Life</em> says the Cambrian was a lottery: replay the tape and you get a different biosphere. Conway Morris says the opposite: replay it and you get eyes, wings, jointed limbs, and crabs again, because the attractors are real. Blount, Lenski, and Losos (2018) reconciled them in <em>Science</em> — contingency dominates at large historical scales, convergence at functional ones. Both, on different axes, are right.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">Contingency vs Convergence</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Why do all the frontier models look alike? Two defensible answers.</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"diptych\">\n        <div class=\"dip spec\">\n          <div class=\"diph\">Gould — Contingency</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">Replay the tape and you get a different frontier. The convergence is mostly imitation.</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">Everyone copies everyone: arXiv preprints, leaked recipes, open weights, and a talent pool that rotates through every lab.</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">Pull one contingent event — no \"Attention Is All You Need\" in 2017, no ImageNet moment in 2012, no cheap CUDA GPUs after 2006 — and the architecture tree grows differently.</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">SSMs, diffusion LMs, and JEPA prove the roads not taken are real, and passable.</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"dip deci\">\n          <div class=\"diph\">Conway Morris — Convergence</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">The attractor is real. The shape is forced by the problem itself.</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">Self-attention is the Mount Fuji of sequence modeling — nothing else yet solves long-range coherence and parallel training at once. (Parallel across the sequence <em>during training</em>; generation is still autoregressive, one token after another.)</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">MoE is the attractor of sparse scaling: selective routing gives the best FLOP-per-parameter ratio.</div>\n          <div class=\"di\">RLHF/RLAIF is the only scalable way to pour human preference into a gradient; chain-of-thought-plus-RL is the only lever we have on the new test-time-compute axis.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">After Gould, <em>Wonderful Life</em> (1989) and Conway Morris, <em>Life's Solution</em> (2003); reconciled by Blount, Lenski &amp; Losos 2018, <em>Science</em>.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>I lean Conway Morris on the model layer and Gould on the ecosystem layer. The architecture will keep converging, probably for several more years, because the attractors are real. But the winners are radically contingent: Anthropic could have been any of the five or six 2020-era alignment-research labs; Cursor could have been any of two dozen AI-IDE startups that didn't quite get the UX loop right. The transformer itself was going to happen — Bahdanau, Cho, and Bengio's 2015 paper was already attention bolted onto an RNN, and pure attention was the natural next move. But <em>Hari-writes-a-piece-about-you</em> happening is not.</p>\n\n    <p>And the tape-of-life crowd is right about one thing: other roads exist, and they are still being walked.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"fig\">\n      <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n        <div class=\"fig-title\">The Roads Not Taken</div>\n        <div class=\"fig-sub\">Alternative architectures — alive, and no longer only at the edges</div>\n      </div>\n      <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n      <div class=\"creature-grid\">\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">State-space models</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Mamba · RWKV · Hyena</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <path d=\"M16 42 Q38 14 60 32 Q82 50 104 30 Q126 12 148 32 Q160 42 172 30\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"42\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"32\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"104\" cy=\"30\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"148\" cy=\"32\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n            <path d=\"M166 24 l8 6 -8 6\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Linear-time sequence modeling · constant memory · a recurrent state stream, not full attention · <strong>Gu &amp; Dao 2023</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Alive <span class=\"p\">|</span> long-context &amp; edge efficiency</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Diffusion LMs</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Iterative denoising · 2025</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <g fill=\"#89a7cc\">\n              <circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"14\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"40\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"30\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"20\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"26\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"44\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n            </g>\n            <path d=\"M52 28 l16 0 M62 22 l8 6 -8 6\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n            <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"174\" y2=\"28\" stroke=\"#4f74ad\" stroke-width=\"1\" opacity=\"0.35\"/>\n            <g fill=\"#4f74ad\">\n              <circle cx=\"104\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"122\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"158\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"174\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\"/>\n            </g>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Generate by denoising a whole sequence at once rather than left-to-right · parallel decoding · went from curiosity to contender in 2025</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Emerging <span class=\"p\">|</span> latency-sensitive uses</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">JEPA</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">LeCun · I-JEPA / V-JEPA 2</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <rect x=\"12\" y=\"20\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4f74ad\" opacity=\"0.85\"/>\n            <rect x=\"148\" y=\"8\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#89a7cc\" opacity=\"0.9\"/>\n            <text x=\"27\" y=\"33\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"white\">x</text>\n            <text x=\"163\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#2c2c2c\">y</text>\n            <circle cx=\"95\" cy=\"30\" r=\"13\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n            <text x=\"95\" y=\"33\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"7.5\" fill=\"#345583\">latent</text>\n            <path d=\"M42 29 Q64 30 80 30\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n            <path d=\"M148 18 Q124 24 110 27\" stroke=\"#89a7cc\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,2\"/>\n            <path d=\"M76 27 l6 3 -6 3\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" fill=\"none\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Predicts a target's <strong>embedding</strong>, not its tokens · non-generative self-supervision · an explicit rejection of the autoregressive crab</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Research frontier <span class=\"p\">|</span> world models</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"cc\">\n          <div class=\"cc-name\">Hybrids</div>\n          <div class=\"cc-tag\">Jamba · Granite 4.0 · Codestral Mamba</div>\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 190 56\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n            <rect x=\"30\" y=\"14\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n            <rect x=\"48\" y=\"14\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/>\n            <rect x=\"66\" y=\"14\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n            <rect x=\"84\" y=\"14\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/>\n            <rect x=\"102\" y=\"14\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n            <rect x=\"120\" y=\"14\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/>\n            <rect x=\"138\" y=\"14\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n          </svg>\n          <div class=\"cc-facts\">Interleave attention (blue) and state-space (tan) blocks · pushed hardest by open-weight labs · <strong>creeping toward the frontier</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"cc-size\">Production-shipping <span class=\"p\">|</span> AI21 · IBM · Mistral</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"fig-cap\">Sources: Gu &amp; Dao 2023 (Mamba); AI21 Jamba, IBM Granite 4.0, Codestral Mamba (attention–SSM hybrids); diffusion-LM work, 2025; LeCun's I-JEPA (2023) and V-JEPA 2 (2025).</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>For now, these live at the edges — long-context efficiency, edge inference, world-model research — rather than at the frontier. But the boundary is porous. The strongest challengers are the hybrids that interleave attention and state-space blocks, and it is the open-weight labs — AI21, IBM, Mistral, and the sprawling Chinese open-source zoo — pushing them fastest. In an ecosystem this young, calling something \"niche\" dates faster than the hardware.</p>\n\n    <p>There is a third reading, though — the one I actually hold.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"callout\">\n      <div class=\"callout-tag\">The reading I actually hold</div>\n      <p>Carcinization is a signal of ecological maturity. Lineages converge hard when the environment has stopped rewarding experimentation — you get crabs <em>after</em> the great decapod radiations, not during them; eyes converge once you live in a bright, well-lit world; C4 photosynthesis converges once you are CO&#8322;-limited. If the AI architecture space is carcinizing, that is the tell that we have left the explosive-speciation phase of the Cambrian and slid onto the downslope — into something Ordovician, where diversity keeps accumulating but disparity, the sheer breadth of body plans, quietly narrows.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>Which takes us, finally, to the future.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"end-orn\">· · ·</div>";

export const S6_HTML = "<p class=\"lead drop-cap\">When Darwin sailed home from the Galapagos, the specimens that would eventually undo him were the least glamorous things in the hold — a drab clutch of little brown finches. Thirteen-odd species, all descended from a single ancestral flock blown out from the mainland, each wearing a different beak: deep and nut-cracking on one island, slender and insect-probing on the next, long and cactus-piercing on a third. One body plan, radiating into every open niche the archipelago had to offer. Biologists call the move <em>adaptive radiation</em>, and it belongs to a quieter chapter than the Cambrian's body-plan-inventing spasm — the later act, when the phyla are already fixed and evolution turns from inventing forms to filling rooms. That is the act the AI ecosystem is walking into now. And since the forward-looking section is the one everyone reads first anyway, I'll stop pretending to be modest about it.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Short-run (2026–2028): the niches fill</h3>\n\n<p>The ecosystem stops producing new body plans and starts producing new species on the ones it already has — the Ordovician move. After the Cambrian invented the phyla, the Ordovician filled the niches: trilobites diversified massively, cephalopods radiated, the first jawless fish appeared. No new phyla; a great many new families. Translated: through 2028 I don't expect a new architecture to overthrow the transformer stack. I expect the frontier-lab count to settle at three apex Western labs — OpenAI, Anthropic, Google DeepMind — plus one or two Chinese apex labs (DeepSeek, Qwen, maybe Kimi), and beneath them a radiation of specialized, niche, vertically-integrated species.</p>\n\n<p>Those species are the finches. Cursor has no ambition to be Claude; it is evolving the sharpest possible beak for a single food source — writing code, with a frontier model running underneath. Harvey feeds on dense contracts, growing a fine, precise instrument for extracting defensible answers where a general model fumbles the jurisdiction. Sierra reaches deeper still: the long probing beak of a customer-service employee that holds policy across a hundred turns, logs what it did, and closes the ticket at the bottom of the conversation. Same ancestral chassis; different beaks; different islands.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">The Finch Radiation</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">Adaptive radiation into niches — the application layer's Ordovician move</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"creature-grid\">\n    <div class=\"cc\">\n      <span class=\"cc-tag\">DEV WORKFLOW</span>\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 92 64\" width=\"92\" height=\"64\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"ground finch with heavy beak\">\n        <line x1=\"6\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"86\" y2=\"53\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <path d=\"M22 41 L5 31 L24 37 Z\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n        <ellipse cx=\"42\" cy=\"34\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"62\" cy=\"23\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <path d=\"M70 17 L90 24 L70 30 Z\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"63\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#fff\"/>\n        <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"48\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <line x1=\"58\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n      </svg>\n      <div class=\"cc-name\">Geospiza cursoria</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-facts\">Heavy, high-torque beak. Cracks the hardest kernel in the ecosystem — the edit-run-debug loop — and eats little else.</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-size\">Niche: IDE-native coding</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cc\">\n      <span class=\"cc-tag\">LEGAL SUBSTRATE</span>\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 92 64\" width=\"92\" height=\"64\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"warbler finch with slender beak\">\n        <line x1=\"6\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"86\" y2=\"53\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <path d=\"M22 41 L5 31 L24 37 Z\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n        <ellipse cx=\"42\" cy=\"34\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"62\" cy=\"23\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <path d=\"M70 21 L92 23 L70 26 Z\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"63\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#fff\"/>\n        <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"48\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <line x1=\"58\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n      </svg>\n      <div class=\"cc-name\">Certhidea harveyi</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-facts\">Fine, precise beak for extracting one thing cleanly: defensible answers from dense contracts. Precision over force.</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-size\">Niche: legal workflow</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cc\">\n      <span class=\"cc-tag\">SERVICE EMPLOYEE</span>\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 92 64\" width=\"92\" height=\"64\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"cactus finch with long probing beak\">\n        <line x1=\"6\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"86\" y2=\"53\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <path d=\"M22 41 L5 31 L24 37 Z\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n        <ellipse cx=\"42\" cy=\"34\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"62\" cy=\"23\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <path d=\"M70 20 L91 27 L70 27 Z\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"63\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#fff\"/>\n        <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"48\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <line x1=\"58\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n      </svg>\n      <div class=\"cc-name\">Camarhynchus sierrae</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-facts\">Long probing beak for multi-turn work — reaching deep into a conversation, holding policy, closing the ticket at the bottom.</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-size\">Niche: customer service</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cc\">\n      <span class=\"cc-tag\">CLINICAL DOCS</span>\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 92 64\" width=\"92\" height=\"64\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"tree finch with medium beak\">\n        <line x1=\"6\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"86\" y2=\"53\" stroke=\"#b3a48a\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <path d=\"M22 41 L5 31 L24 37 Z\" fill=\"#345583\"/>\n        <ellipse cx=\"42\" cy=\"34\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"62\" cy=\"23\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#4f74ad\"/>\n        <path d=\"M70 19 L90 25 L70 28 Z\" fill=\"#c4a882\"/>\n        <circle cx=\"63\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#fff\"/>\n        <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"48\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        <line x1=\"58\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"57\" stroke=\"#345583\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n      </svg>\n      <div class=\"cc-name\">Platyspiza abridgei</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-facts\">Generalist beak tuned to one clinical food source: turning the ambient noise of a doctor's visit into a structured note.</div>\n      <div class=\"cc-size\">Niche: medical scribe</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Adaptive radiation: one ancestral form — the transformer — diversifying into beak-specialized niches. Company examples illustrative; finch archetypes after Darwin's Galapagos ground, warbler, cactus, and tree finches.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">The selective pressure flips</h3>\n\n<p>Once a body plan is fixed, the whole character of competition changes. In the Cambrian, the selective pressure was <em>existence</em> — could you survive the coevolutionary arms race at all? By the Ordovician it had become <em>niche fit</em> — could you do your one particular job better than whatever incumbent already sat in your niche? The AI ecosystem is sliding along that same gradient right now, and once the architecture stops moving, competition stops being about architecture. It collapses onto a handful of measurable axes. In biology, with the arthropod body plan frozen, selection went to work on speed, sensory acuity, camouflage, prey-capture efficiency. Here are the AI equivalents.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">The Five Selective Axes</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">What competition optimizes once the body plan is frozen</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"cat-grid\">\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <div class=\"cat-n\">01</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Token efficiency</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">How many tokens does an agent burn to reach a correct answer? Read that as <em>metabolic efficiency</em>, and the biology is literal. Energetic cost is one of the oldest selective pressures there is: a nervous system is metabolically expensive, and the human brain alone eats roughly a fifth of the body's resting energy — a bill that has shaped everything about how brains are wired. The agent that hits the lowest cost per unit of useful cognition wins the niche.</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-stat\">DeepSeek V3: $0.14 / $0.28 per M tokens — 20–50× cheaper</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <div class=\"cat-n\">02</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Context efficiency</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">How much of the context window gets used <em>productively</em>? Long context is cheap to advertise and expensive to use well. The winners spend context the way a surgeon spends an incision — minimal, deliberate, every token earning its place.</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-stat\">Llama 4 Scout's \"10M tokens\" advertised ≠ 10M tokens used well</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <div class=\"cat-n\">03</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Latency</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">Operator, ChatGPT Agent, Computer Use, Manus — all of them have a latency problem. A human-workflow-replacement agent that takes 45 seconds per click will not replace humans at scale.</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-stat\">~45s per action prices you out of the niche entirely</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <div class=\"cat-n\">04</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Reliability (pass^k)</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">Sierra's τ-bench measures whether an agent solves a task <em>and</em> whether it does so reliably across repeated attempts. The single most underrated competitive axis — it is what Jensen Huang means when he talks about \"nine nines\" of inference.</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-stat\">Early τ-bench pass^8 for GPT-4-era ≈ 25%</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cat-card\">\n      <div class=\"cat-n\">05</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-title\">Time horizon</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-body\">How long a task can an agent carry autonomously before it needs a human? METR's Kwa et al. (2025) clocked the 50% task-completion horizon doubling roughly every four to seven months — a doubling law of its own.</div>\n      <div class=\"cat-stat\">~50 min (early '25) → &gt;14 hrs (late '25) → month-scale by '28?</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\">Selective axes after the body plan freezes. Sources: DeepSeek public pricing; Yao et al., τ-bench (arXiv:2406.12045, 2024); Kwa et al./METR (arXiv:2503.14499, 2025). The Opus 4.5 &gt;14-hr figure is the author's forward claim.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">Task-Completion Time Horizon</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">METR's 50% task-completion horizon for frontier models, on a compressed scale</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"bc-wrap\">\n    <div class=\"bc-row\">\n      <span class=\"bc-label\">Mid 2024</span>\n      <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:9%;background:var(--fig-mid)\"><span>~8 min</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"bc-row\">\n      <span class=\"bc-label\">Early 2025</span>\n      <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:26%;background:var(--blue-light)\"><span>~50 min · Claude 3.7 Sonnet</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"bc-row\">\n      <span class=\"bc-label\">Late 2025</span>\n      <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:58%;background:var(--blue)\"><span>&gt;14 hrs · Opus 4.5</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"bc-row\">\n      <span class=\"bc-label\">2028 (proj.)</span>\n      <div class=\"bc-track\"><div class=\"bc-fill\" style=\"width:100%;background:var(--fig-warm)\"><span>month-scale · if the trend holds</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\"><em>The horizon roughly doubles every 4–7 months.</em> Source: Kwa et al. (METR), \"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Tasks,\" arXiv:2503.14499 (2025). Late-2025 and 2028 bars are the author's forward figures; scale is compressed for legibility.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>There is a second loop running underneath all of this, and it has already started turning: agents selectively reproduce. In a multi-agent ecosystem, whichever agents produce better outputs at lower cost get used more, gather more training signal, get more tuning, and pull further ahead. It is a Darwinian loop that operates on product usage rather than on weights — selection iterated on real users, in real time. Cursor gathers more feedback signal in a day than most frontier-lab pretraining runs see in months. That dogfooding loop is its moat: the beak that cracks its niche's food fastest raises the most chicks.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Medium-run (2028–2032): the agents go to work</h3>\n\n<p>This is where Dario Amodei's \"Machines of Loving Grace\" (October 2024) and Leopold Aschenbrenner's \"Situational Awareness\" (June 2024) earn their place. I won't endorse their timelines — Amodei's \"compressed 21st century\" of biology by ~2030 is aggressive, and Aschenbrenner's AGI-by-2027 is more aggressive still. What they get right is the question: what happens when cognition becomes cheap, abundant, and arbitrarily deployable at machine speed? Jensen Huang, at the January 2025 CES keynote, put it as well as anyone — \"The age of AI Agentics is here. AI agents are the new digital workforce. The IT department of every company is going to be the HR department of AI agents.\" Sam Altman, in his January 2025 \"Reflections\": \"in 2025, we may see the first AI agents 'join the workforce' and materially change the output of companies.\"</p>\n\n<p>I won't underwrite the specific dates. The direction, though, is durable. A measurable fraction of knowledge work in 2028 will be performed by agents — and those agents will be orchestrated by protocols (MCP for tool access, A2A for inter-agent negotiation) that were open-sourced and donated to the Linux Foundation in 2025. Call it the TCP/IP of cognition. That is the organ system getting standardized, and standardized organ systems are what let a body plan scale.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <div class=\"callout-tag\">THE DECIMATION IS OVERDUE</div>\n  <p>Gartner's June 2025 call: \"Over 40% of agentic AI projects will be canceled by end of 2027.\" Its analyst Anushree Verma reckons only about 130 of the thousands of agentic-AI vendors are real. Carnegie Mellon and Salesforce's <em>TheAgentCompany</em> benchmark (2025) finds agents clearing only ~30–35% of multi-step real knowledge-worker tasks. Call that <em>Hallucigenia</em>-era performance — a weird, half-working proto-form — and take heart from it. Decimation is how phyla get made. By 2028 those numbers should be an order of magnitude better, and most of today's vendors will be gone.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>Why do I keep saying <em>three</em> apex Western labs? Because the ingredients of an apex lab — frontier-scale compute contracts, ten-figure capital, and a critical mass of the few thousand people on Earth who can train a frontier model — concentrate viciously. Ecosystems rarely support many apex predators; the energy budget at the top of a food chain is small. But apex predator is one durable seat among several, which is where the open-weight Western labs come in. Meta's Llama, Mistral, and the emerging neo-labs building open models occupy the <em>commons</em> — the substrate niche. They are less the shark than the reef: the shared structure everything else grows on. Their moat lives in the governance and license landscape around open weights — the Linux-Foundation-hosted protocols, the evolving license terms — which I'd argue is the single most under-appreciated moat in the whole ecosystem.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Long-run provocation (2030+): the next phylum</h3>\n\n<p class=\"lead\">Here I am going to take the gloves off.</p>\n\n<p>I think the next truly new phylum — a genuinely new body plan, a step past transformer-plus-reasoning — arrives via a route nobody in the current frontier labs is explicitly optimizing for. New phyla arise when a mature developmental toolkit meets open ecospace. The transformer's toolkit is mature; the permissive ecospace opens when compute keeps getting 10× cheaper every two-to-three years, when agent-native economic actors begin owning resources, and when the training substrate shifts from \"text on the internet\" to \"traces of agents interacting with real environments.\" In that world the selective pressure moves off next-token prediction entirely and lands on long-horizon reliability under novel conditions.</p>\n\n<p>My unreasonable bet: the architecture that wins the 2030s frontier is a hybrid. An SSM-like efficient-context backbone (lineage: Mamba, RWKV) carrying a transformer-attention layer for the critical reasoning steps; a world-model objective in the spirit of JEPA doing the heavy lifting of prediction-in-representation-space rather than in tokens; an external memory-and-tool substrate hosted by MCP-like protocols; and RL on real-world trajectories as a first-class training signal that does not yet exist at any meaningful scale. The Cambrian architecture was self-attention. The Ordovician architecture may be attention-plus-recurrence-plus-world-model-plus-tool-use-as-training-signal.</p>\n\n<p>Or I might be wrong. Stephen Jay Gould would tell me I am wrong in specific ways. Simon Conway Morris — the Cambridge paleontologist who co-described many of the Burgess Shale's strangest fossils and who argued, against Gould, that evolution keeps arriving at the same forms because deep functional constraints act like attractors — would tell me the eventual architecture arrives no matter which lab stumbles into it first. Replay the tape and you still get eyes, still get wings, still get jointed limbs. The survival-of-the-fittest story for today's companies is, I think, brutal but legible.</p>\n\n<div class=\"fig\">\n  <div class=\"fig-hd\">\n    <div class=\"fig-title\">Survival of the Fittest, 2026–2036</div>\n    <div class=\"fig-sub\">A brutal-but-legible prediction table</div>\n  </div>\n  <hr class=\"fig-divider\"/>\n  <div class=\"diptych\">\n    <div class=\"dip spec\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">Likely to survive the decade</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">The three or four apex labs — OpenAI, Anthropic, Google DeepMind, plus DeepSeek and Qwen.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">The infrastructure oligopoly — NVIDIA, TSMC, ASML — with the hyperscalers as distribution channels.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Vertical winners with proprietary workflow data — Sierra, Harvey, Abridge, Cursor/Windsurf, Perplexity.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">The agent protocols — MCP, A2A — as infrastructure substrate.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">The dark horse: open-weight governance and the license landscape — the most under-appreciated moat in the ecosystem.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"dip deci\">\n      <div class=\"diph\">Likely to die or consolidate</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Generic wrappers without distribution — the first-gen Jasper cohort is already gone.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Most early multi-agent frameworks — LangChain pivoted, Swarm was replaced by the Agents SDK, CrewAI and Autogen consolidate into 2–3 winners.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Most voice-clone startups, and the bulk of \"AI agent for X\" where X is a common horizontal function.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">Devin's exact current form — Cognition survives, the specific demo product morphs.</div>\n      <div class=\"di\">The <em>Anomalocaris</em> cohort — the impressive apex predators of 2023 may not all make it.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"fig-cap\"><em>Decimation is how phyla form.</em> Author's forecast; company examples illustrative. The survive/decimate split mirrors the Cambrian-to-Ordovician pruning that turned early disparity into a handful of durable lineages.</p>\n</div>\n\n<p>There is a third column I won't dignify with a table, because it is genuinely high-variance: agents as economic actors with their own compute budgets and wallets (technically feasible, socially uncertain); AI-to-AI markets via A2A with real price discovery (promising, immature); persistent digital entities with year-long memory running autonomously (Park et al.'s 2023 \"Generative Agents\" is the technical template, though nothing runs at scale yet). And then there are the extinction-event risks — the Cretaceous–Paleogene meteor analog that would compress this whole picture into a smoking crater: a prompt-injection-driven breach at a frontier lab; a regulatory hammer from Brussels or Beijing that caps frontier training; a power-grid bottleneck as 10GW-class clusters hit physical limits; or a genuine capability plateau, the DeepSeek-shock story where scaling quietly stops paying off. If I had to price \"the current ecosystem persists roughly as-is for ten years,\" I'd put it in the low double digits. \"Extinction-event-class disruption before 2032,\" also low double digits. The most probable outcome is the boring one: continued Ordovician radiation, continued protocol standardization, continued decimation of the mid-tier, a handful of Big-Five-scale outcomes, and the slow emergence of a post-Cambrian stable ecosystem.</p>\n\n<div class=\"end-orn\">· · ·</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"sub\">Coda — back to the museum</h3>\n\n<p>Which brings me, improbably, back to the Cambrian Hall. I think a lot about why I loved that room as a kid. Partly the weirdness — the form-space of life turning out to be so much stranger than the books had prepared me for. But also, I think now, the mixture of contingency and pattern. You could look at <em>Anomalocaris</em> and know its lineage was dead; you could look at <em>Pikaia</em> and know its lineage was, weirdly enough, you. But you could not have known that at the time. You had to wait 520 million years to find out.</p>\n\n<p>We do not have that luxury. We are writing investment memos, forming companies, deciding where to work and what to study, in the middle of the \"Cambrian\" — while the weird wonders are still swimming around us, before the decimation, before the radiation, before the protocols standardize and the apex predators settle and the carcinization hardens. We are choosing which body plan to bet on. Most of us, statistically, will back the <em>Anomalocaris</em> that doesn't make it. Some of us will accidentally back the <em>Pikaia</em> that does. Gould's move, at the end of <em>Wonderful Life</em>, is humility: you cannot predict which is which. Conway Morris's move, in <em>Life's Solution</em>, is ambition: the attractors are real and discoverable if you stare hard enough at the constraints. I believe both. The phyla of AI are visible now and will be durable; the species within them are radically contingent; and the apex predators of 2026 are probably not all the apex predators of 2046.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"pull\">The most important capability of the next two decades is the ability to read the ecosystem the way paleontologists read the Burgess Shale — to tell a durable body plan from a transient oddity, an apex predator from the quiet ancestor of everything that comes next.</blockquote>\n\n<p>That is what a VC does. It is, increasingly, what a founder does. And it is what I am trying to learn to do.</p>\n\n<p>I walked out of the Museum of Natural History that first time with a photo of a trilobite. I still have it in the archives of my photo albums. <em>Phacops rana</em>, Devonian, 380 million years old. It survived 270 million years of the Paleozoic. It did not survive the end-Permian extinction, which wiped out 96% of marine species — including every trilobite that had ever lived. AI systems may enjoy a long run as the ecosystem's leaders. Maybe they won't. But if the trilobite settled anything, it is that a hell of a run is a run worth having. And that is the best any tech-loving enthusiast could ask for!</p>\n\n<p class=\"lead\" style=\"text-align:right\"><em>A Food For Thought,<br/>— Hari</em></p>\n\n<div class=\"end-orn\">✦</div>";

